Interface Internals:
Conceptually, an interface value carries two pieces of information:
1. Dynamic type — the concrete type currently stored.
2. Dynamic value — the concrete value currently stored.

var s Speaker = Dog{Name: "Bruno"}
 - Dog is Dynamic type stored
 - Dog{Name: "Bruno"} is Dynamic value stored
 - s.Speak(), then Dog's speak implementation is invoked and this is called as Dynamic Dispatch
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Empty interface — interface{} and any
 - An empty interface has no methods: interface {}
 - Since every Go type satisfies an interface that requires zero methods, an empty interface can hold values of any type.
 - In Go 1.18 and later, any is an alias for interface{}
 - var value any
   value = 10
   value = "hello"
   value = true
   value = []int{1, 2, 3}
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Type assertions:
 - A type assertion extracts or verifies a concrete type stored inside an interface.
 - var v any = "hello"
   s := v.(string)
   fmt.Println(s) // hello
 - type Closer interface {
       Close() error
   }
   
   func CloseIfSupported(v any) error {
       c, ok := v.(Closer)
       if !ok {
           return nil
       }
   
       return c.Close()
   }
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Type switches:
A type switch is useful when you need different behavior for multiple possible dynamic types.

func Describe(v any) {
    switch x := v.(type) {
    case string:
        fmt.Println("String:", x)

    case int:
        fmt.Println("Integer:", x)

    case bool:
        fmt.Println("Boolean:", x)

    default:
        fmt.Printf("Unknown type: %T\n", x)
    }
}
